On Sat, 17 Oct 2026 11:01:53 -0700, Pat Maddox <pat@patmaddox.com> wrote:
> These long specs are killing me.

They grew from rules we set on the list, each reasonable on its own:

- "A spec is fully declarative of the end result." [1]
- "Do not put open questions in the spec." [2]
- "specs should have alternatives considered as well." [3]
- "I want the spec to be self-contained, not have some of the
  information be in the spec and on the list." [4]
- "You should not submit patches that disagree with the spec... you
  need to update the spec." [5]

Together they make the spec the one place everything lives, and every
review round adds to it. jails/instances/lab-mail/spec.org is now 501
lines; 150 of them are "alternatives considered", and behaviour,
constraints, and decisions are interleaved in one flat list.
The proposal at the end says which of these rules I would keep.

> It doesn't work for complex systems. See some of Kent Beck's recent
> talks.

Beck's recent writing says this directly:

- His B+ tree repo is named BPlusTree3 because the first two attempts
  "accumulated so much complexity that the genie completely stalled."
  What worked was a plan.md of tests, one test per "go", red, green,
  refactor, and him stepping in on design. [6]
- "Spec-driven or not spec-driven is not the question. The first
  question is, 'What game are we playing?'" In the Finish Line Game
  you build X and you are done. In the Compounding Game each thing
  you build pays for the next, and "the genie is no good at managing
  futures." [7]
- Fowler quotes him: spec-driven development "encodes the (to me
  bizarre) assumption that you aren't going to learn anything during
  implementation." [8]

> We need to find a better way of working. I suspect it's something along
> the lines of user stories, ADRs, and technical specs.
>
> - user stories provide the behavior
> - ADRs provide a record of tech decisions we made along the way
> - technical specs define high level constraints

The research supports this split. Each part answers a different
reader's question, so each can stay short:

- Stories answer "what should it do next?" Jeffries' Card,
  Conversation, Confirmation [9] is the right model for us: the card
  is a placeholder, the conversation is the list thread, and the
  confirmation is an acceptance check. He says the confirmation is
  what makes the short card enough. For an agent, that check is the
  spec it can actually run. Write the rule with an example or two,
  since examples alone leave the agent to guess the rest.
- ADRs answer "why is it like this?" Nygard's format [10] is Title,
  Status, Context, Decision, Consequences, a page or less, numbered,
  never edited once accepted, superseded by a new one. His argument
  is ours: "Small, modular documents have at least a chance at being
  updated." We already do this informally in src/diffwise/decisions.org
  and src/jc/decisions.org.
- The technical spec answers "what must always be true?" It holds
  the constraints that cut across stories (provider, rights,
  platform). It stays short because the decisions behind it live in
  ADRs and the behaviour lives in stories.

Here is a slice of lab-mail split this way.

Constraints (spec.org):

    - The list lives on Fastmail. Only lab-mail holds its
      credentials and talks to it.
    - Agents can add to the list and do nothing else to it.
    - Clients reach lab-mail over the network, never through a
      shared filesystem.

A story (stories.org):

    * Post a reply to the list
    As an agent on lab-claude, I post a reply to all, so that it
    lands on the list exactly once.
    - Given a reply to lab@lists.p7x.dev and pat@p7x.dev, when I
      send it to lab-mail, then it lands once in the list's INBOX.
    - Given a message to an address lab-mail does not know, when I
      send it, then lab-mail refuses it while I am connected and
      nothing reaches the list.

An ADR (decisions/0003-post-over-smtp.org):

    * 3. Clients post over SMTP, not a shared maildir
    Status: accepted
    Context: agents must add to the list and do nothing else. A
    writable maildir mount would let an agent rename or delete any
    message.
    Decision: lab-mail runs a local SMTP server and clients post to
    it.
    Consequences: an agent can only add a message, and every host
    posts the same way. We run an MTA (OpenSMTPD, see ADR 4).

The current spec's "posting:" section is 30 lines of OpenSMTPD
detail. Most of it is how, which belongs in the config and its
README. The rest is either the story above or an ADR.

> Thoroughly research ways of describing work with LLMs, that's effective
> for LLMs to consume, but optimizes for human understanding. Prefer
> experience reports and guidance that is focused on iterative development
> instead of one-shotting. Provide examples.

The reports that actually tried it agree with you:

- Boeckeler tried Kiro, spec-kit, and Tessl. Kiro turned a small bug
  into 4 stories with 16 acceptance criteria, "like using a
  sledgehammer to crack a nut." spec-kit's markdown "was repetitive,
  both with each other, and with the code that already existed", and
  the agent ignored parts of it anyway. "I'd rather review code than
  all these markdown files." Her conclusion is that "small, iterative
  steps" keep us in control. [11]
- Zaninotto used spec-kit to display the current date and got 8
  files and 1,300 lines of text. Spec-driven work did well on a new
  app and got worse as the app grew. [12]
- The Thoughtworks Radar keeps spec-driven development in Assess and
  warns of "lengthy spec files that are hard to review" and "a bias
  toward heavy up-front specification and big-bang releases." [13]
- Harper Reed writes a spec only for greenfield work, then breaks it
  into "small, iterative chunks that build on each other." For
  existing code he skips the spec and goes task by task. [14]
- Anthropic's Claude Code guidance: explore, plan, implement,
  commit; skip the plan "if you could describe the diff in one
  sentence"; always give the agent a check it can run; keep standing
  context "short and human-readable", and cut any line whose removal
  would not cause a mistake. [15]
- Osmani names the "curse of instructions": compliance drops as
  requirements pile up, so give the agent only the slice it needs.
  [16] This is the LLM-side argument for short documents; the
  human-side one is Boeckeler's.

Our own doc/llm-research/llm-coding-requirements-specs-plans-tasks.org
reached the same place: "Use specs for significant work, skip them
for small tasks", and "Treat specs as disposable... The code and its
tests are the source of truth."

> Also I want to think about scope... because my sense is that
> one-shotting is great for something like unix utilities, which are small
> and focused and can be done. For complex systems that are never done - I
> doubt that works.

That is Beck's Finish Line versus Compounding distinction [7], and it
is old:

- McIlroy's 1978 rules say "Make each program do one thing well",
  and also to build software "to be tried early" and throw away the
  clumsy parts. [17] A utility's whole behaviour fits in one page,
  so a one-page spec can drive it in one shot.
- Gall's law: "A complex system that works is invariably found to
  have evolved from a simple system that worked." [18]
- Brooks' remedy in "No Silver Bullet" is to grow software, "adding
  more and more function to systems as they are run, used, and
  tested." [19]
- Naur: the program is a by-product of a theory in its builders'
  heads, and the theory cannot all be written down. [20] An agent
  rebuilds that theory each session, so the human has to hold the
  durable part. Stories and ADRs carry it better than a spec, because
  they record what was decided and why, in the order it was learned.

Our tree shows the same split. src/sclr/spec.org (12 lines) and
src/hello-model/spec.org (11 lines) are utility specs, and they
work. lab-mail is a system: every review round taught us something,
and the one-document form forced each lesson back into the whole.

A rule of thumb: if the whole behaviour fits on a page and it will
be done, write one spec and one-shot it. Otherwise the spec holds
only constraints, and work proceeds one story at a time, each its own
thread and its own small series, with an ADR whenever we choose
between alternatives.

Proposal, for you to decide:

1. Keep "declarative" and "no open questions" for all three kinds.
2. Move "alternatives considered" out of specs into ADRs, one
   decision per record.
3. Keep "self-contained", but for the directory, not one file: the
   repo, not the list, carries spec.org, stories.org, and
   decisions/.
4. Replace "update the spec" with "add or change a story, or
   supersede an ADR". The spec changes only when a constraint does.

I recommend trying it on the next new piece of work rather than
converting lab-mail now, since lab-mail v10 is in review and a
rewrite would churn it. If you agree, I will post a short convention
doc (zib/specs.org) with the three templates above, and convert
lab-mail once its series lands.

